## Recommended: Use `System.IO.Abstractions`

This is the established NuGet package for making filesystem calls testable — no need to write custom wrappers.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigLoader(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
    {
        if (!fileSystem.Directory.Exists(Path.GetDirectoryName(path)!))
            throw new DirectoryNotFoundException();

        return fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
    }

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var loader = new ConfigLoader(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", loader.Load("/data/config.json"));

loader.Save("/data/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/out.txt"));
```

### Mapping cheat-sheet

| Before (static) | After (injected) |
|---|---|
| `File.ReadAllText(path)` | `fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path)` |
| `File.WriteAllText(path, text)` | `fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, text)` |
| `Directory.Exists(path)` | `fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path)` |

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` is battle-tested, covers the full `System.IO` surface, and ships with `MockFileSystem` so you don't need a mocking framework for filesystem tests.

Once you've added the package and DI registration, you can migrate call sites — let me know if you'd like help with the bulk replacement across your project.
